# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in real values.
# `.env.local` is gitignored. This file is committed and must never contain secrets.
#
# Every variable the application reads should be listed here, even if optional.
# Keep it current: an out-of-date .env.example is worse than none.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACTIVE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Canonical public origin, no trailing slash.
# Used for absolute URLs in metadata, Open Graph tags, sitemap.xml and robots.txt.
# Local:      http://localhost:3000
# Production: https://bidiptaroy.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FILE STORAGE (Vercel Blob) — required from Phase 9 onward for uploads
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Project images and resume PDFs are stored here. Create the store in the
# Vercel dashboard: Storage → Create → Blob, then copy the read/write token.
# `vercel env pull` writes it into .env.local for you.
#
# Optional in the sense that the site runs without it: with no token, the
# upload forms in /admin are replaced by a message saying storage is not
# configured, and everything else — including images uploaded earlier —
# works normally. Uploads are the only thing that stop.
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=vercel_blob_rw_XXXXXXXXXXXX

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRANSACTIONAL EMAIL (Resend) — contact form notifications, Phase 9b
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Optional, and the form is built so that it stays optional. Without a key,
# messages are still validated, rate-limited, and STORED — they are readable
# at /admin/messages, and both the dashboard and the inbox say that nothing
# was emailed. The database is the record; email is a notification on top.
#
# Sign up at resend.com and create an API key.
#
# ⚠ Until you verify a domain, Resend only sends FROM onboarding@resend.dev
#   and only TO the address your Resend account was registered with. Register
#   with bidiptar@bu.edu and notifications work; register with anything else
#   and they will be silently dropped while the API reports success. Domain
#   verification lands with the custom domain in Phase 11.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_XXXXXXXXXXXX
#
# Where notifications go. Defaults to the email on the Profile row, which is
# already published on the site, so leaving this unset is a sane default.
# CONTACT_TO_EMAIL=
#
# The From address. Leave unset until a domain is verified.
# CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL="Portfolio <onboarding@resend.dev>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE (Neon Postgres) — required from Phase 6 onward
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Neon gives you two connection strings for the same database. They are not
# interchangeable, and swapping them causes failures that look like outages:
#
#   DATABASE_URL — the POOLED string. Its host contains "-pooler".
#                  Used at runtime by the app. Serverless functions open many
#                  short-lived connections; without the pooler they exhaust
#                  Postgres's connection limit under modest traffic.
#
#   DIRECT_URL   — the DIRECT string, without "-pooler" in the host.
#                  Used by `prisma migrate` and `prisma db seed`, which hold a
#                  session open. A transaction pooler cannot support that, and
#                  migrations will fail or hang against the pooled URL.
#
# In the Neon console: Connect → copy the pooled string, then toggle
# "Connection pooling" off and copy the direct one.
#
# ⚠ WHICH BRANCH. As of Phase 11 there are three Neon branches, one per
#   purpose, and the values below must come from the DEVELOPMENT one:
#
#     production   → the live site. Set in VERCEL ONLY, Production scope.
#                    Its connection string should not be on your machine.
#     development  → local `npm run dev`, `db:seed`, `db:reset`, Studio.
#                    THIS is what DATABASE_URL and DIRECT_URL below are.
#     e2e          → `npm run e2e`. The E2E_* variables above.
#
#   Before Phase 11 there was one database and `.env.local` pointed at the
#   live site — which meant `npm run db:seed` reverted production to whatever
#   was in git, and `npm run db:reset` destroyed it outright. Both commands
#   are now safe because of what they point at, not because they changed.
#   See docs/decisions/0012.
#
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require"
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxx.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# END-TO-END TESTS (Playwright) — Phase 10b. Only needed to run `npm run e2e`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ⚠ These MUST NOT point at the database above.
#
# The e2e suite signs in and creates, publishes and deletes real rows — that is
# what it is for. Right now `.env.local` and the Vercel project share ONE Neon
# database, so DATABASE_URL is the live site's content. `/projects` is served
# dynamically, so a test project published against it would appear on the
# public site, and stay there if a run crashed before its cleanup.
#
# `npm run e2e` refuses to start unless E2E_DATABASE_URL is set, and refuses
# again if it resolves to the same database as DATABASE_URL — comparing hosts
# with "-pooler" normalised away, so the pooled and direct strings for one
# database are correctly recognised as the same place.
#
# The easiest throwaway database is a Neon branch:
#   Neon console → Branches → New branch → copy its two connection strings.
# It is a copy-on-write clone, so it costs almost nothing and can be reset or
# deleted freely. The suite migrates and seeds it on every run, so it does not
# matter what state it is left in.
#
# CI does not use these values — .github/workflows/ci.yml sets them itself
# against a throwaway Postgres service container, and deliberately leaves
# DATABASE_URL unset in that job.
#
# E2E_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
# E2E_DIRECT_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxx.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTHENTICATION (Auth.js v5) — required from Phase 7 onward
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Signs and encrypts the session cookie. Generate with:
#   npx auth secret          (writes it into .env.local for you)
# or:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Changing this value invalidates every existing session, which is also how you
# force yourself signed out everywhere if a device is lost.
# AUTH_SECRET=
#
# AUTH_URL is not needed on Vercel — it is inferred there. Set it when hosting
# anywhere that does not provide the deployment URL, and ALSO when running a
# production build locally:
#
#   AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000 npm start
#
# Without it, `npm start` answers every /api/auth/* request with 500 and logs
# `UntrustedHost: Host must be trusted`, because the automatic inference is
# Vercel-specific. `npm run dev` is unaffected, which is why this is easy to
# miss until you test a signed-in flow against a production build.
# AUTH_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT YET USED — added in later phases. Listed so the full surface is visible.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# (Nothing pending. Phase 10 added no variables; Phase 11 adds production
# database credentials and a custom domain.)
#
# Note for CI: .github/workflows/ci.yml sets DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_URL,
# AUTH_SECRET and NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL itself, against a throwaway Postgres
# service container. BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN is left unset there on purpose, so
# that the build keeps proving it works without file storage configured.
